I have been learning a bit about inverse probability weights (IPW) from two posts by Andrew Heiss. I tried a few changes to simplify / improve upon the method he uses in his post, so I thought I would write them up here.
IPW uses two models to account for confounders that affect both the probability of receiving a treatment and the distribution of an outcome variable that is influenced by the treatment. The first model (the treatment model) produces a set of weights that are applied to each observation in the second model (the outcome model). This is done to up-weight observations that are “surprising” to the treatment model. For a more thorough explanation, see the posts linked above. They’re good.
In the latter post, Dr. Heiss tries to take a fully Bayesian approach that uses a distribution of weights rather than a fixed value for each observation. This is good, but (I think) we can do better. I see two related spots with room for improvement:

Don’t bother with the custom C++ counter injection business. This is over-complicated and un-Stannic (is that the right adjectival?). You’re already at the “writing raw Stan” party, so you might as well just estimate the weights in the Stan program.
Estimate the treatment model (and hence the weights) simultaneously with the outcome model. This means calculating the weights in the transformed parameters block. When they are treated as data you’re behaving as if the treatment model posterior as “fixed”. Generally I prefer to get a full joint distribution of the entire system.

This also has the potential to change the results since the joint distribution of weights and coefficients need not have the same marginals.
One could argue that you don’t want the outcome model to influence the treatment model estimates. I’m on the fence about that. If that bothers you you could in principle add priors to diminish that effect.


So let’s try it. We’ll use the same (synthetic) dataset and models:

Treatment model: net ~ income + temperature + health
Outcome model: malaria_risk ~ net with weights.

This is what the data looks like:


