Notably, we get slightly different estimates:

The treatment model coefficients are consistent in sign and relative magnitude, but slightly smaller than before.
a lower (more negative) estimate of the effect of nets on malaria risk (oc_coef). It’s now about -14 instead of -10.
and a slightly lower estimate of baseline malaria risk (oc_intercept). -35 instead of -39.

I haven’t done a deep dive into understanding these differences, but it’s good that they’re consistent. Apparently the true right answer from the original simulation is about -10, so I may be making some mistake here. My best guess at the moment is that the weights on the outcome model sort of puts those observations on a higher scale, so the joint posterior tends to shift toward whatever yields the strongest fit on the outcome model. But that’s just a guess. More to dig in on in the future maybe, but that’s all for now!

